The 56 declarative fixtures under gate/, decide/, sequence-a/ and sequence-c/ and the
seven canonical byte vectors under canonical/ were promoted — copied unchanged in id,
file name and content — from the TypeScript reference implementation.

  repository  Sakwala/affiant-ts   https://github.com/Sakwala/affiant-ts
  commit      aed3bfb045e21c65b978ed1b5d661fee7905ecad
  package     @affiant/core 0.1.0-alpha.0
  path        packages/core/test/fixtures/{gate,decide,sequence-a,sequence-c,canonical}
  runner      packages/core/src/testing.ts, published as @affiant/core/testing
  promoted    2026-09-04

One document was re-promoted after the first promotion, from `c4591ea` to the commit above:
`sequence-a/typed-inputs-on-the-card`. Its `expect.card.fields[]` stated `allowedValues` and
`pattern`, which is where the reference implementation carried them before it was aligned to
this version's wire; v0.1 puts both on the card envelope, in `presentation`, so the fixture
named keys a v0.1 card does not have and every driver had to map them. It now states
`expect.card.presentation[]`, and `conformance/RUNNER.md` §4.2 and
`conformance/fixture.schema.json` were changed with it. That was possible only because no
version tag existed yet: a parity manifest cites a fixture by id, so one produced against one
set of bytes says nothing about another. Every run published under conformance/results/ names
on its own record the ref it was produced against, which is how a reader tells which set of
bytes a given claim is about.

Byte-identical, and deliberately so. A parity manifest cites a fixture by its id, so a
rename silently changes what a published document refers to; and a fixture edited on
the way in would no longer be the document the reference implementation is green
against, which is the only thing that makes "this implementation passes it and that one
does not" a comparison rather than an opinion.

The format these documents are written in is conformance/RUNNER.md; the schema they are
checked against is conformance/fixture.schema.json; the index that says which rules each
one checks and which of them must fail against a release known to be broken is
conformance/fixtures/MANIFEST.json, section "conformance".

To verify the promotion, from a checkout of both repositories:

  diff -r <affiant-ts>/packages/core/test/fixtures/gate       conformance/fixtures/gate
  diff -r <affiant-ts>/packages/core/test/fixtures/decide     conformance/fixtures/decide
  diff -r <affiant-ts>/packages/core/test/fixtures/sequence-a conformance/fixtures/sequence-a
  diff -r <affiant-ts>/packages/core/test/fixtures/sequence-c conformance/fixtures/sequence-c
  diff -r <affiant-ts>/packages/core/test/fixtures/canonical  conformance/fixtures/canonical
